<h2 class="bde-heading-506-128 bde-heading">
Why Do the Nets Have Grant Nelson on a Two-Way Deal?
</h2><div class="bde-fancy-container-506-129 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-fancy-container-506-145 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-f-text-506-158 bde-f-text">
<p>Nelson is on a two-way contract because he went undrafted, and a seven-footer without a draft position starts at the bottom like everyone else.</p>
<ul>
<li>He’s 7’0″ and runs the floor, which is the physical profile that keeps clubs looking even when the production isn’t there.</li>
<li>He’s listed at 215 lbs, which is light for the height, and adding strength is the obvious requirement.</li>
<li>He went undrafted in 2025, so no club had any obligation to him at all.</li>
<li>His only published earnings before this year came from a ten-day contract in February worth <strong>$73.15k</strong>.</li>
<li>Two-way contracts don’t count against the cap, which is why a club can carry him at no cost to its books.</li>
</ul>
<p>A full season on a two-way is a considerable step up from ten days in February.</p>

</div><div class="bde-shortcode-506-159 bde-shortcode"><div class="nba-fin" data-nba-fin="why">
